PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The panel maintains full consensus that identifying a PLL case under CFOP requires 5 non-top-facing stickers mathematically and 6 practically. Since OLL fixes piece orientations, observing one lateral sticker per piece determines its identity. Information theory and parity constraints prove that 4 stickers are insufficient (256 < 288 legal states), while 5 stickers (covering 3 corners and 2 edges) uniquely resolve the remaining pieces and AUF via parity closure. In standard speedcubing practice, 2-sided recognition naturally presents 6 lateral stickers across two adjacent faces due to the shared corner showing two colors. Both the theoretical minimum of 5 and the practical standard of 6 are fully retained.
}

6.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The deliberation confirms total consensus across both the theoretical and practical dimensions of the problem. Mathematically, 4 stickers are strictly insufficient by the Pigeonhole Principle ($4^4 = 256 < 288$), whereas 5 stickers (observing 1 lateral sticker on 3 corners and 2 edges) are necessary and sufficient to determine all piece positions and AUF through parity conservation. Practically, CFOP 2-sided recognition utilizes 6 stickers across two adjacent faces, which encompasses those same pieces along with one redundant corner sticker. The final response should clearly present 5 as the strict information-theoretic minimum and 6 as the practical CFOP speedcubing standard.
}

